const FAQS = [
  {
    "q": "What is AI agent observability?",
    "a": "The practice of tracking cost, latency, errors, and behavior from LLM-powered agents using the telemetry they already produce, so you know what an agent did and what it cost without guessing after the fact."
  },
  {
    "q": "How is monitoring AI agents different from traditional monitoring?",
    "a": "Traditional monitoring assumes fixed costs and errors that announce themselves. Agents break both: cost varies per call, and failures often look like a successful response, so you need cost and behavior tracking, not just uptime and error rate."
  },
  {
    "q": "Do I need OpenTelemetry to monitor my AI agents?",
    "a": "Not strictly, but it's the open standard most agent frameworks already emit spans in, so you avoid re-instrumenting everything later if you switch backends or add new models."
  },
  {
    "q": "How do you catch a runaway AI agent loop before it gets expensive?",
    "a": "Watch call volume against the agent's own recent baseline. A sharp jump with no matching increase in completed work is the classic sign of a loop that isn't converging."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is AI agent security the same as AI agent observability?",
    "a": "No, but they share the same telemetry. Observability asks what an agent did and what it cost; security asks whether any of it was an attack, like a prompt injection or a hijacked loop."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is prompt injection and why is it hard to detect?",
    "a": "Content the agent reads, a document, a ticket, a tool result, contains hidden instructions that hijack its behavior. It's hard to catch because every resulting action can look legitimate on its own; only the untrusted input plus the behavior shift gives it away."
  },
  {
    "q": "Can I monitor agents across multiple LLM providers in one view?",
    "a": "Yes, if your pipeline reads standard GenAI attributes rather than one vendor's API format, swapping or mixing models doesn't fragment your dashboard."
  },
  {
    "q": "Should agent telemetry live in a separate tool from the rest of my monitoring?",
    "a": "No. Keeping it siloed means manually jumping between tools to tell whether a slowdown is the model or a downstream dependency. One timeline removes that step."
  }
